Physical design — a Sales star walkthrough.

Enough theory — let's read a real one. Here is the Jabra Sales star, table by table, as it would actually be built.

Start at the centre: FACT SALES. Its grain is one order line, and its columns fall into three groups. There's the surrogate primary key, sales key. There are the foreign keys out to every dimension — order-date key, customer key, product key, channel key, promotion key. There are a few degenerate dimensions — order i-d, order-line i-d, order status — which have no table of their own. And there are the measures — quantity, unit price, discount, tax, and line total. Foreign keys out, a few degenerates, and the additive measures — that's the whole fact.

Now the dimension points. Dim date — one row per day, a year-month-day key, calendar and fiscal attributes; the universal, role-playing dimension. Dim customer, a slowly-changing type-two dimension — with its surrogate key, its natural customer i-d, name, segment, and geography — city, province, region, country — plus the history columns: effective date, expiry date, and is-current. Dim product, also type-two — surrogate key, natural product i-d, name, product line, category, brand, list price, plus its history columns. Dim channel — channel key, channel name like Web, App, Amazon, or Retail, and channel type. And dim promotion — promotion key, name, discount type, discount value.

Finally, the design choices worth naming. One: a surrogate key on every table — stable, source-independent, fast integer joins. Two: natural keys kept as attributes, customer i-d and product i-d, so you can trace a row back to the source. Three: slowly-changing type-two columns on customer and product, because they change over time and need history — that's module six. Four: audit columns — insert date and update date — on the dimensions, recording when ETL loaded or changed each row. And five: degenerate dimensions — order i-d and order status — living on the fact, with no table. A physical star is unglamorous and repeatable: a fact of foreign keys, degenerates, and measures, ringed by surrogate-keyed dimensions that keep their natural keys, carry history columns where needed, and audit stamps throughout.
